# Untitled

In Situ Encapsulation of Atomically Precise Nanoclusters in Reticular Frameworks via Mechanochemical Synthesis
The combination of atomically precise nanoclusters (APNCs) and reticular frameworks is promising for generating component-specific nanocomposites with emergent properties. However, traditional liquid-phase synthesis often hampers this potential by damaging APNCs and limiting combination diversity. Here, mechanochemical synthesis to explore the encapsulation of diverse oil-and water-soluble APNCs within various reticular frameworks is employed, establishing a database of 21 unique APNC—framework combinations, including metal—organic frameworks (MOFs), covalent—organic frameworks (COFs), hydrogen—bonded organic frameworks (HOFs), and multivariate MOFs. These framework coatings not only spatially immobilize APNCs but also secure their structures, preventing aggregation and degradation while enhancing stability and activity. Encapsulating \(\mathsf{A u}_{25}\) in HOFs resulted in a remarkable 315-fold increase in catalytic activity compared to \(\mathsf{A u}_{25}\) homogeneous catalyst, highlighting the framework's crucial role in catalytic enhancement. The mechanochemical synthesis strategy facilitates tailored support screening, catering to specific needs, and shows promise for developing multifunctional systems, including enzyme—APNC@frameworks material for cascade reactions.
## 1. Introduction
With the aim of forming precise and customizable nanocomposite structures, extensive studies have been conducted to elucidate specific structure—property relationships within complex systems, facilitating molecular-level design flexibility.[1,2] Atomically precise nanoclusters (APNCs) and reticular frameworks, such as metal–organic frameworks (MOFs), covalent–organic frameworks (COFs), and hydrogen—bonded organic frameworks (HOFs), feature crystallographically defined structures. These structures have found independent applications in fields such as catalysis, fluorescence, and biomedicine with adjustable architectures.[3–8] Combining APNCs with reticular frameworks offers synergistic advantages, yielding component-specific nanocomposites.[9,10] In contrast to conventional adsorption on the surface of crystalline frameworks, integrating APNCs into reticular frameworks leads to robust spatial confinement
within 3D frameworks, greatly enhancing APNC stability while concurrently improving accessibility and selectivity.[11,12] Notably, reticular frameworks provide APNCs with well-defined pore structures and customizable inner-surface environments, facilitating the elucidation and optimization of synergistic interactions between APNCs and frameworks.[13–15]
Several approaches have been employed to integrate APNCs into reticular frameworks. The infiltration of APNCs into framework channels has achieved the homogeneous encapsulation of \(\mathrm{Au}_{133}(\mathrm{SR})_{52}{}^{+}\) within MOF crystals.[16] However, this method is limited to a couple of frameworks with large pore sizes for APNC passing through. In contrast, in situ formation of reticular frameworks around presynthesized APNCs circumvents pore size restrictions and achieves immobilization within well-ordered framework crystals.[17–19] This approach effectively prevents APNC aggregation and enhances both stability and performance.[20,21] Fischer's group illustrated the spatial confinement of APNCs within ZIF-8, protecting \(\mathrm{Pt}_{12}\) from agglomeration after ligand removal.[22] Yuan's group demonstrated that the ability of ZIF-8 to restrict ligand vibrations and self-quenching effects notably increases the electrochemiluminescence efficiency of APNCs.[23] Jiang's work encapsulated \(\mathrm{Au}_{25}\) within various UiO66 frameworks, altering the inner surface microenvironment
for electronic state and reactivity optimization.[24] These studies underscore the vast potential of APNC−framework combinations across multiple domains; however, the diversification of such nanocomposites remains limited, hindering their versatility and controllability. This limitation primarily arises from traditional liquid-phase framework synthesis conditions,[25,26] which involve organic solvents, high temperatures, and extended durations, rendering them incompatible with APNCs and often resulting in structural damage. Therefore, a universal method for fabricating diverse APNC@framework nanocomposites under mild conditions and in a short time is highly desirable but challenging. Alternatively, the mechanochemical preparation of frameworks has been applied in various fields, such as nanoparticle immobilization,[27,28] biomedical delivery,[29] and gas adsorption,[30] notably for encapsulating enzymes and enhancing their catalytic activity.[31,32] This strategy offers an efficient approach for synthesizing frameworks at ambient temperature in a solid-state process within a few minutes,[33–35] allowing the preservation of the APNC structure during framework formation.
In this study, we present a novel mechanochemical strategy aimed at significantly expanding the synthesis range of APNC@framework composites, resulting in the creation of a comprehensive database comprising 21 unique nanocomposites. The inherent incompatibility issues between frameworks and APNCs, including solvent, temperature, and precursor mismatches, are resolved using a solid-state mechanochemical encapsulation process conducted at room temperature. Diverse oil- and water-soluble APNCs, featuring varying charges, ligands, and metal cores, are successfully incorporated into a diverse range of reticular frameworks, such as MOFs, multivariate MOFs, and notably, COFs and HOFs, for the first time. This integration offers extensive structural complexity and functional versatility to the APNC@framework material. The newly developed framework coatings not only spatially confine their structures, significantly enhancing APNC stability under challenging conditions, but also provide specific microenvironments for immobilized APNCs. Through optimization of the framework matrix, \(\mathrm{Au}_{25}@\mathrm{HOF}-101\) demonstrates a remarkable 315-fold increase in catalytic efficiency in enzyme-mimicking reactions compared to free \(\mathrm{Au}_{25}\). Furthermore, a multifunctional \(\mathrm{GOx}/\mathrm{Au}_{25}@\mathrm{UiO}-66\) \(\mathrm{GOx}=\) glucose oxidase) was constructed since the immobilized process is mild enough even for natural enzymes, showing a 3.6-fold enhancement in activity compared to homogeneous mixtures. This innovative methodology shows promise for the flexible selection of both framework matrices and APNC species in APNC@framework systems, thereby facilitating the on-demand construction and regulation of component-specific nanocomposites for various applications.
## 2. Results and Discussion
## 2.1. Conventional Framework Synthesis Conditions Destabilize APNCs
To confirm the general instability of free APNCs under conventional framework synthesis conditions and hence the necessity of a new approach, we first aimed to determine the stability of APNCs under those conditions. The ultrastable sil
ver nanocluster \(\mathrm{Ag}_{44}(p\mathrm{-}\mathrm{MBA})_{30}{}^{4-}\) \(\left(p\mathrm{-}\mathrm{MBA}=p\right)\) -mercaptobenzoic acid, denoted as \(\mathrm{Ag}_{44}\) hereafter) was used as the model APNC and was tested in the typical solvents (water, methanol, and ethanol) and temperature at \(80~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\), which are used for framework synthesis.[36] The results revealed pronounced aggregation and total degradation of APNCs under these conventional framework synthesis conditions (Figures S1 and S2, Supporting Information).
In addition to the framework synthesis conditions, the precursor building blocks of reticular frameworks, such as organic linkers and metal nodes, sometimes destroy embedded APNCs. To demonstrate such incompatibility, we chose ZIF-8, a benchmark of the reticular frameworks,[37,38] for this study. After adding ZIF linker 2-methylimidazole, the characteristic peaks of \(\mathrm{Ag}_{44}\) disappeared, and a new peak at \(404~\mathrm{nm}\) was observed, suggesting the destruction of APNCs (Figure 1a). In addition, the metal node of ZIF-8 adversely affected APNC structures, leading to partial aggregation (Figure S3, Supporting Information). Thus, because of the instability of APNCs during traditional framework synthesis, the diversity of APNC@framework nanocomposites is highly limited.
By avoiding high temperatures and minimizing the use of organic solvents during synthesis, the mechanochemical strategy effectively addresses the thermal and solvent incompatibility challenges associated with APNCs.[39–41] The solid-state mechanochemical process, along with rapid encapsulation, significantly reduces exposure to framework precursors, helping to preserve APNC structures.[42–44] Additionally, APNCs in the solid state are more stable than those in solution.[45] Consequently, in situ assembly of APNC@framework nanocomposites via mechanochemical synthesis emerges as a promising strategy.
## 2.2. Synthesis and Characterization of \(\mathbf{A g}_{44}@\mathbf{Z}\mathbf{I}\mathbf{F}-\mathbf{8}\)
To evaluate the feasibility of mechanochemically integrating APNCs into frameworks, the mechanochemical stability of the APNCs was investigated. The results suggest that mechanochemical processes below \(480\;\;\mathrm{rpm}\) had no impact on the APNC structures (Figure 1b; Figures S4–S7, Supporting Information). Higher mechanochemical intensities resulted in better ZIF crystallinity; however, beyond \(480~\mathrm{rpm}\), the improvement in framework crystallinity became negligible (Figure S8, Supporting Information). Additionally, ZIF crystallinity improved significantly with the addition of milling balls, whereas almost no ZIF-8 coating formed in their absence (Figure S9, Supporting Information). Time-dependent framework formation showed that the milling balls dramatically facilitated the formation of the ZIF shell, confirming that the process is driven by mechanochemistry (Figure S10, Supporting Information).[46]
\(\mathrm{Ag}_{44}@\mathrm{ZIF}-8\) nanocomposites were synthesized by adding ZIF precursors (2-methylimidazole and \(\mathrm{ZnO}\)) and \(\mathrm{Ag}_{44}\) to a zirconia grinding jar and grinding the mixture at \(480\;\;\mathrm{rpm}\) for 5 min (Figure 1c). Wide angle X-ray scattering (WAXS) and powder X-ray diffraction (PXRD) patterns of \(\mathrm{Ag}_{44}@\mathrm{ZIF}-8\) matched well those of ZIF-8 without any extra peaks, suggesting the incorporation of \(\mathrm{Ag}_{44}\) did not alter the long-range ZIF-8 crystal structure (Figure 2a; Figure S11, Supporting Information). X-ray
photoelectron spectroscopy (XPS) of \(\mathrm{Ag}_{44}@\mathrm{ZIF}{-}8\) revealed distinct peaks of \(\mathrm{Ag}\) and S elements from \(\mathrm{Ag}_{44}\) , indicating the presence of \(\mathrm{Ag}_{44}\) in ZIF-8 crystals (Figure S12, Supporting Information). The \(\scriptstyle{C=0}\) stretching vibration peak, attributed to free APNCs, was observed in the FTIR spectrum of \(\mathrm{Ag}_{44}@\mathrm{ZIF}{-}8\) (Figure 2b). Thermogravimetric analyses (TGA) revealed more weight loss in \(\mathrm{Ag}_{44}@\mathrm{ZIF}{-}8\) than pure ZIF, which was possibly attributed to the embedded \(\mathrm{Ag}_{44}\) (Figure S13, Supporting Information). Inductively coupled plasma mass spectrometry (ICP−MS) suggested the loading capacity of \(\mathrm{Ag}_{44}@\mathrm{ZIF}{-}8\) was \(1.12\,\mathrm{wt.}\%\) . All analyses confirm the successful encapsulation of \(\mathrm{Ag}_{44}\) within the ZIF-8 frameworks.
The structural integrity of embedded \(\mathrm{Ag}_{44}\) was investigated next. In the UV–vis diffuse reflectance spectra, the characteristic peaks of \(\mathrm{Ag}_{44}@\mathrm{ZIF}{-}8\) were the same as those of free \(\mathrm{Ag}_{44}\) , indicating that the atomic configuration and geometric structure of encapsulated \(\mathrm{Ag}_{44}\) were highly preserved (Figure 2c). High-angle annular dark-field scanning transmission electron microscopy (HAADF-STEM) images showed that the embedded \(\mathrm{Ag}_{44}\) was uniformly dispersed in ZIF8 (Figure 2d). TEM images revealed no significant change in the APNC sizes, implying that \(\mathrm{Ag}_{44}\) was not aggregated after being loaded into ZIFs. (Figure S14, Supporting Information).
The distribution of \(\mathrm{Ag}_{44}\) in ZIFs was explored. The \(\mathrm{N}_{2}\) uptake and surface area of \(\mathrm{Ag}_{44}@\mathrm{ZIF}{-}8\) were slightly lower than those of pure ZIF-8 (Figure S15, Supporting Information), demonstrating the presence of \(\mathrm{Ag}_{44}\) in ZIF crystals. However, the pore
size of ZIF-8 was not large enough for accommodating \(\mathrm{Ag}_{44}\) , suggesting that \(\mathrm{Ag}_{44}\) was embedded into the ZIF scaffold or adsorbed on the ZIF surface. The fitted synchrotron small angle X-ray scattering (SAXS) patterns with a hierarchical structural model showed the presence of mesopores in \(\mathrm{Ag}_{44}@\mathrm{ZIF}{-}\) 8 with a radius of \(1.49\,\pm\,0.01\,\mathrm{\nm}\) (Figure 2e), which were sufficiently large to accommodate isolated \(\mathrm{Ag}_{44}\) molecules (radius: \(1.39\ \mathrm{nm}\) , Figure S16, Supporting Information). However, such mesopores were not detected in pure ZIF-8 (Figure S17, Supporting Information). The presence of hierarchical pores in \(\mathrm{Ag}_{44}@\mathrm{ZIF}{-}8\) could be attributed to the occupation of \(\mathrm{Ag}_{44}\) within ZIF crystals, emphasizing that \(\mathrm{Ag}_{44}\) was embedded into the ZIF skeleton.[47,48]
To further investigate the distribution of \(\mathrm{Ag}_{44}\) in ZIFs, we compared \(\mathrm{Ag}_{44}@\mathrm{ZIF}{-}8\) , produced via the mechanochemical method, with \(\mathrm{Ag}_{44}\) on-ZIF-8, produced through physical absorption. Physical absorption resulted in only \({\approx}30\%\) of the initial APNC being successfully immobilized (Figure 3a; Figure S18, Supporting Information), whereas the mechanochemical strategy achieved a loading efficiency of \(99\%\) for \(\mathrm{Ag}_{44}@\mathrm{ZIF}{-}8\) . Washing experiments demonstrated minimal leaching of APNCs from \(\mathrm{Ag}_{44}@\mathrm{ZIF}{-}8\) , while about half of the \(\mathrm{Ag}_{44}\) in \(\mathrm{Ag}_{44}\) on-ZIF-8 was lost. The total loading ratio of \(\mathrm{Ag}_{44}@\mathrm{ZIF}{-}8\) was \(90\%\) of the initial APNCs after washing, which is six times greater than that of \(\mathrm{Ag}_{44}\) -onZIF-8 \((15\%)\) , indicating more robust interactions between APNC and ZIFs in \(\mathrm{Ag}_{44}@\mathrm{ZIF}{-}8\) . These different interactions were further confirmed by FTIR and XRD data. \(\mathrm{Ag}_{44}@\mathrm{ZIF}{-}8\) showed a slight shift in the \(\scriptstyle{C=0}\) stretching vibration peak (Figure S19,
Supporting Information) and a smaller crystallite size \((20.59\,\mathrm{nm})\) compared to surface-adsorbed \(\mathrm{Ag}_{44}\) -on-ZIF-8 \((26.75\,\mathrm{nm})\). The loading capacities of other APNCs@ZIF-8 also exceeded \(80\%\), contrasting sharply with the less than \(30\%\) loading for APNCs-on-ZIF-8 (Figures S20–S23, Supporting Information), suggesting that various APNCs and frameworks all adopted an embedded form. Additionally, HAADF-STEM images revealed distinct differences between \(\mathrm{Ag}_{44}@\mathrm{ZIF-8}\) and adsorbed \(\mathrm{Ag}_{44}\) -on-ZIF-8. In \(\mathrm{Ag}_{44}@\mathrm{ZIF-8}\), the embedded \(\mathrm{Ag}_{44}\) was uniformly incorporated throughout the ZIF-8 structure, whereas in the adsorbed \(\mathrm{Ag}_{44}\) - on-ZIF-8, the \(\mathrm{Ag}_{44}\) was only distributed across portions of the ZIF surfaces (Figure S24, Supporting Information). The embedded encapsulation form provides more robust protection for APNCs. HAADF-STEM images of \(\mathrm{Ag}_{44}@\mathrm{ZIF-8}\) showed no significant changes in \(\mathrm{Ag}_{44}\) size after thermal treatment at \(250\ ^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\), while \(\mathrm{Ag}_{44}\) -on-ZIF-8 exhibited noticeable Ag agglomeration (Figure 3b–e). Finally, treatment with ionic liquid (IL) revealed that \(\mathrm{Ag}_{44}@\mathrm{ZIF-8}\) remained unchanged, while surface-adsorbed \(\mathrm{Ag}_{44}\) in \(\mathrm{Ag}_{44}\) -on-ZIF-8 was almost completely decomposed within \(10\;\mathrm{\min}\) (Figure S25, Supporting Information). These results collectively confirm that the mechanochemical strategy successfully enables the encapsulation of APNCs within the framework.
## 2.3. Enhanced Stability of \(\mathbf{Ag}_{44}@\mathbf{Z}\mathbf{IF-8}\)
Enhancing the robustness of functional APNCs would broaden their applicability in catalysis and fluorescence studies, addressing a major challenge in their industrial utilization.[49,50] Herein, the ability of ZIF coatings to protect \(\mathrm{Ag}_{44}\) from extreme environments was examined. After the removal of the coordinating solvent in APNC crystals, free \(\mathrm{Ag}_{44}\) completely destructs. In contrast, \(\mathrm{Ag}_{44}@\mathrm{ZIF-8}\) highly retained the morphology and geometric structure of \(\mathrm{Ag}_{44}\) after the coordinating solvent removal (Figure 3f). When exposed to the ionic liquid, free \(\mathrm{Ag}_{44}\) immediately decomposed because of the structural disturbance. Remarkably, the embedded APNCs were intact in the presence of IL, exhibiting almost the same optical absorption spectrum (Figure 3g). In the chemical tolerant experiment, the same systems were treated with \(1\,\mathrm{~M~}\,\mathrm{NH}_{4}\mathrm{OH}\). Once again, \(\mathrm{Ag}_{44}@\mathrm{ZIF-8}\) maintained its natural structure after treatment, and free \(\mathrm{Ag}_{44}\) was thoroughly destroyed (Figure 3h). Thermal stability was investigated by immersing free \(\mathrm{Ag}_{44}\) and \(\mathrm{Ag}_{44}@\mathrm{ZIF-8}\) in boiling DMF. As expected, free \(\mathrm{Ag}_{44}\) aggregated to form larger Ag nanoparticles, while \(\mathrm{Ag}_{44}@\mathrm{ZIF-8}\) remained unchanged (Figure 3i). We have demonstrated that \(\mathrm{Ag}_{44}\) is securely embedded within the defected cavities of ZIFs, leading to the spatial
confinement of APNCs. These ZIF enclosures effectively preserve the geometric structure of embedded APNCs at the atomic level, preventing their migration and aggregation,[9] thereby providing unparalleled protection to APNCs.
## 2.4. Extension of APNCs@Frameworks via the Mechanochemical Strategy
Having demonstrated the successful encapsulation and enhanced stability of \(\mathrm{Ag}_{44}@\mathrm{ZIF-8}\), we proceeded to explore the scope of APNCs to assess the generalizability of our strategy. (Figure 4a). The combination of oil-soluble APNCs, including \([\mathrm{Au}_{4}\mathrm{Cu}_{6}(\mathrm{Dppm})_{2}(\mathrm{SAdm})_{4}\mathrm{Cl}_{3}]^{+}\)and \([\mathrm{Ag}_{3}\mathrm{Cu}_{2}(\mathrm{SPhMe}_{2})_{6}]^{-}\), with ZIF-8 was first investigated. PXRD, XPS, and FTIR data revealed that incorporating oil-soluble APNCs did not alter ZIF formation (Figures S26–S28, Supporting Information). The UV–vis spectra of APNCs@ZIF-8 matched well with those of free counterparts, demonstrating the intrinsic structures of APNCs were maintained after immobilization in ZIF-8 (Figure 4b,c). Water-soluble APNCs, such as \(\mathrm{Au}_{25}(\mathrm{L-Cys})_{18}\), \(\mathrm{Pd}_{8}(\mathrm{C}_{4}\mathrm{H}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}\mathrm{S})_{8}\), and \(\mathrm{Ag}_{4}\mathrm{Ni}_{2}(\mathrm{DMSA})_{4}\), were also successfully incorporated within ZIF-8 (Figures S29–S32, Supporting Information). The UV–vis spectra further confirmed the structural integrity of the embedded APNCs (Figure 4d–f).
The surface charge of APNCs plays a crucial role in their incorporation into MOFs.[21,51] Herein, we investigated the in
fluence of APNCs' charge on the encapsulation process. Oilsoluble \([\mathrm{Pd}_{3}(\mathrm{C}_{7}\mathrm{H}_{7})_{2}\mathrm{Cl}_{3}]^{-}\)and \([\mathrm{Pd}_{3}\mathrm{Cl}(\mathrm{PPh}_{2})_{2}(\mathrm{PPh}_{3})_{3}]^{+}\), possessing the same metal core but disparate surface charges, were efficiently immobilized to ZIF-8 without structural loss. This result demonstrates that the mechanochemical strategy is suitable for APNCs with different charges (Figure 4g,h; Figures S33–S35, Supporting Information). Furthermore, the types of building blocks in APNCs, including protected ligands (thiol, phosphorus, alkane, and mixed-ligands) and metal cores (Ag, Au, Pd, Cu, and Ni), had a negligible influence on the immobilization process.
To expand the scope of framework support, various combinations of \(\mathrm{Ag}_{44}@\mathrm{frameworks}\) were tested (Figure 4i). Robust MOFs, such as UiO-66, MOF-74, and HKUST-1, were selected to encapsulate \(\mathrm{Ag}_{44}\). PXRD analysis revealed that no additional peaks were observed for nanocomposites, confirming the preservation of pure MOF topologies following APNC incorporation (Figure 4j–l). The successful encapsulation of \(\mathrm{Ag}_{44}\) in MOFs was further confirmed by XPS, FTIR, UV–vis spectroscopy, \(\mathrm{N}_{2}\) sorption isotherms, and TEM (Figures S36–S44, Supporting Information).
Significantly, our strategy was also employed to immobilize \(\mathrm{Au}_{25}(\mathrm{L-Cys})_{18}\) (denoted as \(\mathrm{Au}_{25}\) hereafter) within COFs and HOFs. The crystallinities of \(\mathrm{Au}_{25}@\mathrm{OOF-TpPa}\) and \(\mathrm{Au}_{25}@\mathrm{HOF-101}\) were comparable to those of the parent frameworks, and the presence of APNCs in the nanocomposites was confirmed by spectroscopic analysis. (Figure 4m,n; Figures S45–S52,
Supporting Information). To our knowledge, this is the first report of in situ embedding APNCs within COFs and HOFs.
Multivariate MOFs (MTV-MOFs) have the capability to incorporate multiple types of functional groups within the same crystal while preserving the framework topology and introducing variations.[52,53] Subsequently, we explored the feasibility of our strategy for embedding APNCs in MTV-MOFs. For multivariate linkers, \(\mathrm{Ag}_{44}@\mathrm{UiO-66-R}\) \(\left(\mathrm{R}=\mathrm{NH}_{2}, \mathrm{NO}_{2}, \mathrm{OH}, \mathrm{OCH}_{3}\right)\) was successfully synthesized (Figure 4c; Figure S53–S60, Supporting Information). For multivariate metal nodes, MOF-74-Zn/Mg-X (X represents the ratio of Mg nodes in the MOFs; \(\mathrm{X}=0.25\) and 0.5) were used to accommodate APNCs. All the resulting nanocomposites exhibited high crystallinity, and the incorporation of APNCs into various MTV-MOFs was subsequently confirmed (Figure 4p; Figures S61–S65, Supporting Information). Overall, this mechanochemical strategy overcomes the compatibility issues associated with the reticular assembly of APNC@framework nanocomposites and offers a versatile approach to significantly diversify the combinations of APNCs and reticular frameworks.
