## 2.5. Catalytic Applications of APNCs@Frameworks Based on Mechanochemical Strategy
The enhanced stability of APNCs has been successfully demonstrated, enabling them to maintain their original structure and catalytic activity across various reaction conditions, thereby
enhancing their robustness and recyclability. \(\mathrm{Ag}_{44}@\mathrm{ZIF-8}\), \(\mathrm{Ag}_{44}\)-on-ZIF-8, and free \(\mathrm{Ag}_{44}\) were applied as catalysts in the cyclization of propargylamine reactions,[54] and their recyclability was extensively investigated. \(\mathrm{Ag}_{44}@\mathrm{ZIF-8}\) exhibited excellent stability and recyclability, demonstrating consistent activity, porosity, and crystallinity over five cycles (Figure 5a; Figures S66–S68, Supporting Information). While free APNCs lost more than \(90\%\) of their activity in three cycles, the reactivity of \(\mathrm{Ag}_{44}\)-on-ZIF-8 gradually decreased with a \(50\%\) activity loss in five cycles. The UV–vis spectrum indicated that free \(\mathrm{Ag}_{44}\) underwent degradation due to the alkaline reaction environment (Figure 5b). Benefiting from the enhanced stability of embedded APNC, \(\mathrm{Ag}_{44}@\mathrm{ZIF-8}\) retained its original structure and catalytic activity after the reaction.
