We further investigated the superiority of mechanochemical strategy in significantly expanding reticular frameworks, facilitating on-demand construction and regulation of nanocomposites. \(\mathrm{Au}_{25}\) was selected as the model clusterzyme for use in the horseradish peroxidase (HRP)-mimicking reaction.[55,56] The
optimal pH for the HRP-mimicking reaction was 4.0 (Figure 5c). Theoretical calculations suggested that \(\mathrm{Au}_{25}\) first absorbs \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}\) (Int1), which subsequently binds free \(\mathrm{H^{+}}\) from the environment \(([\mathrm{Int}2]^{2+})\) to facilitate \(\mathrm{O}-\mathrm{C}\) bond cleavage \(([\mathrm{Int}3]^{2+})\), confirming the necessity of an acid medium appropriate for the reaction (Figure 5d; Figure S69, Supporting Information). However, ZIF-8 immediately decomposed in acidic media \((\mathrm{pH}<6)\), thus unsuitable for the HRP-mimicking reaction. Using our mechanochemical strategy, we found other robust frameworks for encapsulating \(\mathrm{Au}_{25}\), such as UiO-66 and HOF-101 (Figures S70–S73, Supporting Information). \(\mathrm{Au}_{25}@\mathrm{ZIF}-8\) completely broke down in the HRP-mimicking environment, while the on-demand constructed \(\mathrm{Au}_{25}@\mathrm{HOF}-101\) and \(\mathrm{Au}_{25}@\mathrm{UiO}-66\) retained their crystallinity (Figures S74 and S75, Supporting Information).
We compared the catalytic efficiency of free \(\mathrm{Au}_{25}\), \(\mathrm{Au}_{25}@\mathrm{UiO}-66\), \(\mathrm{Au}_{25}@\mathrm{HOF}-101\), and natural HRP enzymes (Table S2, Supporting Information). The Michaelis constant \((K_{\mathrm{m}})\) of \(\mathrm{Au}_{25}@\mathrm{HOF}-101\) was significantly smaller than that of free \(\mathrm{Au}_{25}\), \(\mathrm{Au}_{25}@\mathrm{UiO}-66\), and even HRP, indicating robust substrate affinity (Figure 5e; Figures S76–S78, Supporting Information). The catalytic efficiency parameter \(k_{\mathrm{cat}}/K_{\mathrm{m}}\) for \(\mathrm{Au}_{25}@\mathrm{HOF}-101\) demonstrated efficiencies 315 and 334 times greater than free \(\mathrm{Au}_{25}\) and \(\mathrm{Au}_{25}@\mathrm{UiO}-66\), respectively. The improved catalytic activity of \(\mathrm{Au}_{25}@\mathrm{HOF}-101\) was attributed to the framework regulations, wherein the framework strongly adsorbed the reactant in HOF pores via multiple hydrogen bonds,[57] thereby preenriching the reactant around the APNC catalytic site and dramatically enhancing APNC reactivity.
We also explored the extension of encapsulated species, enabling the development of complex multifunctional systems. A multifunctional catalyst \((\mathrm{GOx}/\mathrm{Au}_{25}@\mathrm{UiO}-66)\) consisting of a natural enzyme and a clusterzyme was synthesized (Figure 5f; Figures S79–S82, Supporting Information). 3D view confocal laser scanning microscopy (3D-CLSM) revealed that GOx was uniformly distributed throughout MOF crystals,[47,53] and conformation analysis confirmed the preservation of the secondary structure of GOx after encapsulation (Figures S83 and S84, Supporting Information). The time-dependent formation of 2,3-diaminophenazine (DAP) by the \(\mathrm{GOx}/\mathrm{Au}_{25}@\mathrm{UiO}-66\) biocatalytic system was compared with that of a homogeneous mixture of GOx and \(\mathrm{Au}_{25}\) at equal concentrations (Figure 5g). The catalytic efficiency of \(\mathrm{GOx}/\mathrm{Au}_{25}@\mathrm{UiO}-66\) in cascade reaction was enhanced \(\approx3.6\)-fold compared with that of homogeneous mixtures. The superiority is attributed to the close integration between enzyme and clusterzyme in UiO-66 crystal,[58,59] resulting in a high local concentration of \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}\) near \(\mathrm{Au}_{25}\). Furthermore, control experiments indicated that single \(\mathrm{GOx}@\mathrm{UiO}-66\) or \(\mathrm{Au}_{25}@\mathrm{UiO}-66\) composites could not trigger the cascade reaction, highlighting the essential role of both GOx and \(\mathrm{Au}_{25}\) in enzyme–clusterzyme cascade catalysis.
## 3. Conclusion
In conclusion, twenty-one APNCs@frameworks were successfully synthesized by using mechanochemical synthesis. This method, which employs a solid-state and room-temperature approach, overcomes compatibility issues associated with framework synthesis conditions, thereby preserving the structural integrity of APNCs during encapsulation. The resulting nanocomposites exhibit significantly improved stability and demonstrate a 315-fold increase in reactivity compared to free APNCs. Importantly, our method dramatically expands the diversity of both APNCs and reticular frameworks, enabling the design of customized nanocomposites and multifunctional systems for specific applications. This diversity allows for the creation of tailor-made nanocomposites capable of targeting precise functions such as HRP-mimicking catalysis and facilitates the construction of biocatalytic cascades with multiple catalytic components. We anticipate that this study presents a promising avenue for developing component-specific nanocomposites and lays the groundwork for designing high-performance materials with enhanced stability and functionality.
